--- Finding (closures_value_capture): a function value held in a record's field
--- and called through it is refused as a function nothing calls.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1):
---   closures_value_capture-1.co:33:1: `square` is handed as a value, and nothing types its parameters —
---   apply it in the function it is handed to, or call it directly once
---     33 │ c = Closure[square, 3]
--- Expected: 9. The spec (Specialization) refuses a function value "nothing
--- calls", and says "a call through a variable that holds it types the
--- parameters it gives" — here `g` holds it and is called. The same refusal
--- for `c.f[c.x]`, for `g = c.f` / `g[c.x]` inside a function taking the
--- record, and for `apply[c.f, c.x]` with `fn apply[f, x]` / `f[x]`; a
--- function value in a plain variable (`g = square`, `g[3]`) runs. With a
--- direct call of `square` anywhere in the program (`log[square[0]]`) the
--- program below runs and prints 9 in both modes, so the record holds the
--- function well: only its typing misses the call through the field.
--- A list of functions grown by `push` is refused the same way where a list
--- literal of them (a group) is not: `fs = push[[], sq]` then `g = fs[0]` /
--- `g[4]`, or `fs = []`, `fs = push[fs, sq]` in a loop, then `for f in fs` /
--- `log[f[4]]` — though the spec counts a loop calling through the list as a
--- call (it refuses a function "held in a list no loop calls through"), while
--- `fs = [sq, cube]`, `for f in fs` / `log[f[3]]` and `fs[0][5]` run.
--- Related: findings/call_a_function-1.co names the record-field case in
--- passing; its own case (a group literal called by position) runs with the
--- compiler swapped in at 19:53, and the cases here still fail with it.
--- Found writing a closure as the pair a compiler makes of it (the function
--- and the value it captured).
struct Closure [f, x]

fn square[i]
    return i * i

c = Closure[square, 3]
g = c.f
log[g[c.x]]
